+### (3.4) Multi-frequency jitter regression ★
+
+**Statement**
+
+```
+r(f) = r₀ + (2π t_j,total)² f²        with r = 10^(−SNR/10)
+t_j,total = √slope / 2π
+```
+
+**Validity** — captures at constant amplitude, identical `f_s`, record length,
+clock route and FFT convention. Strictly better than the two-point (3.1)
+subtraction: the intercept is the baseline, the slope gives the number, and the
+residual reports whether the loss has a jitter signature at all.
+
+**Why this matters in the audio band.** The resulting bound is set by SNR
+*repeatability*, not absolute SNR:
+
+| SNR repeatability | Bounds t_j | Jitter SNR at 20 kHz | vs. a ~90 dB limiter |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0.5 dB | 82 ps | 99.7 dB | +9.1 dB |
+| 0.2 dB | 51 ps | 103.9 dB | +13.3 dB |
+| 0.1 dB | 36 ps | 106.9 dB | +16.3 dB |
+| 0.05 dB | 25 ps | 110.0 dB | +19.4 dB |
+
+At 0.1 dB this bounds total jitter near 36 ps against 25,200 ps from (3.2) —
+roughly three orders of magnitude. It converts the `BOUNDED` tag from an
+assertion into a measured negative result. It still bounds **total** jitter and
+cannot separate the aperture term; see (3.5).
+
+**Class** `VENDOR` · **Source** requires citation (ADI AN-501)
+· **Code** `fit_jitter_from_sweep()`
