+### (4.3) Sigma-delta aliasing is band-limited ★
+
+**Statement** — a sigma-delta converter carries a decimation filter by
+construction, removing essentially everything between `f_s/2` and the modulator
+rate. Folding is confined to narrow bands around `n · f_mod`.
+
+**Consequence.** A vendor claim of "no anti-aliasing filter" means no *analog*
+filter; the digital one cannot be absent. The analog front-end requirement is
+therefore not broadband rejection but adequate attenuation in those narrow bands,
+and a first-order RC suffices:
+
+| RC corner | at 3.072 MHz (64×) | at 6.144 MHz (128×) | droop at 20 kHz |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 50 kHz | 35.8 dB | 41.8 dB | −0.64 dB |
+| 100 kHz | 29.8 dB | 35.8 dB | −0.17 dB |
+| 200 kHz | 23.7 dB | 29.8 dB | −0.04 dB |
+
+**This supersedes the earlier claim** that a multi-pole filter is required; that
+reasoning applied to a Nyquist converter. It also means a folding test must
+inject **at** `n · f_mod` — tones at 25–200 kHz sit where the decimation filter
+already works and would wrongly exonerate the design.
+
+**Unverified.** The oversampling ratio is inferred from convention, not read from
+the converter datasheet. The whole table shifts if the OSR differs.
+
+**Class** `SESSION` — requires datasheet confirmation of the OSR.
+· **Code** `noise_budget()` note
